/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/terraform.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("terraform", [["tf1",0,1,"A team rebuilds a test environment by hand each sprint and the builds never match. Which IaC benefit most directly fixes this?",["Lower cloud list prices for the same resources","Faster network throughput between the servers","Repeatable builds from the same versioned code","Automatic replacement of the operating system"],2,"Code in version control produces the same environment every time. IaC doesn't change prices, bandwidth or OS choice.","Objective 1b",["IaC changes how resources are defined, not what the cloud provider charges for them, so prices stay the same.","Network throughput depends on instance types and network design, not on whether the environment was built by code or by hand.",null,"IaC does not automatically swap operating systems; the OS is whatever image the code specifies, and the problem here is inconsistency between builds."],1],["tf2",0,1,"Which statement best describes Terraform's declarative approach?",["You describe the end state and Terraform works out the steps","You write each API call yourself in the exact order Terraform must run it","You record console clicks and Terraform replays them","You write shell scripts that Terraform schedules"],0,"Terraform configuration declares the desired result; Terraform builds the dependency graph and decides the actions. Listing each call in order is the imperative style.","Objective 1a",[null,"Writing each API call in a fixed order is the imperative style; in Terraform you declare resources and it builds the call order from the dependency graph.","Terraform does not record or replay console clicks; it reads configuration files and calls provider APIs to reach the declared state.","Terraform does not schedule shell scripts; that describes a scripted, imperative approach rather than declaring the desired end state."],1],["tf3",0,1,"A company runs workloads on AWS and Azure and manages DNS with Cloudflare. How can Terraform help?",["It converts AWS resources into equivalent Azure resources whenever asked","It needs a separate Terraform edition for each cloud","It can only manage one provider per state file","One workflow and language manage all three through providers"],3,"Providers let a single configuration and plan/apply workflow cover many platforms. Terraform doesn't translate resources between clouds, and one state can hold several providers.","Objective 1c",["Terraform has no feature that translates AWS resources into Azure ones; each provider has its own resource types that you write separately.","There is one Terraform CLI for every platform; support for each cloud comes from provider plugins, not separate editions.","A single state file can track resources from many providers, such as AWS, Azure and Cloudflare in one configuration.",null],1],["tf4",0,1,"An engineer runs terraform apply twice in a row without changing code or infrastructure. What happens on the second run?",["Every resource is recreated from scratch","Terraform reports no changes to make","Terraform duplicates each resource","The state file is deleted and rebuilt"],1,"Terraform is idempotent: it compares configuration to current state and acts only on differences, so there is nothing to do.","Objective 1b",["Terraform only acts on differences; with no changes to code or infrastructure it has no reason to recreate anything.",null,"Terraform tracks each resource in state and knows it already exists, so a second apply does not create duplicates.","State is not deleted on apply; Terraform reads and updates it, and with no changes it stays effectively the same."],1],["tf5",0,1,"Which task is Terraform designed for, compared with a configuration management tool?",["Editing application config files inside running VMs","Provisioning and managing the lifecycle of infrastructure","Patching operating system packages on long-lived servers every night","Collecting logs from servers and sending alerts"],1,"Terraform provisions and manages infrastructure resources through APIs. Package patching and in-guest configuration are what tools like Ansible or Chef focus on.","Objective 1a",["Editing config files inside running VMs is in-guest configuration, which is the job of configuration management tools like Ansible or Chef.",null,"Nightly OS package patching on long-lived servers is configuration management work, and it also runs against Terraform's immutable-infrastructure approach.","Log collection and alerting is a monitoring or observability function, not something Terraform does (though it can provision the monitoring tools)."],1],["tf6",0,1,"A security lead wants every infrastructure change reviewed before it happens. Which IaC practice supports this?",["Giving every engineer console admin rights","Running terraform apply -auto-approve on laptops","Letting engineers make changes first and write up documentation afterwards","Storing configuration in Git and reviewing plans in pull requests"],3,"Version-controlled code lets changes and their plans be reviewed and approved. Broad console access and auto-approve bypass review.","Objective 1b",["Broad console admin rights let people change infrastructure directly, bypassing any review of the change.","-auto-approve skips the approval prompt, and running from laptops has no shared review step, so this removes review rather than adding it.","Documenting after the fact means the change already happened unreviewed; the requirement is review before the change.",null],1],["tf7",0,1,"What lets Terraform manage services such as GitHub teams or Datadog monitors, not just cloud servers?",["A provider plugin for each service's API","Terraform's built-in SaaS connector","A special Terraform edition built only for SaaS tools","Manual steps recorded in the state file"],0,"Any service with an API can have a provider, which is why Terraform is service-agnostic. There is no special SaaS edition.","Objective 1c",[null,"Terraform has no built-in SaaS connector; SaaS services are supported through provider plugins like any other platform.","There is no SaaS-only Terraform edition; the same CLI manages any service that has a provider.","State records managed objects and their attributes; it cannot perform manual steps or make Terraform able to talk to a new service."],1],["tf8",0,2,"A required_providers entry reads: aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\", version = \"~> 5.40\" }. Which version could terraform init select?",["6.0.0","5.39.0","5.72.1","4.67.0"],2,"`~> 5.40` allows 5.40 and any later 5.x release, but not 6.0. 5.39 is below the minimum.","Objective 2a",["~> 5.40 only lets the minor and patch parts increase, so it excludes the next major version 6.0.0.","5.39.0 is lower than the 5.40 minimum that ~> 5.40 sets.",null,"4.67.0 is an earlier major version and is below the 5.40 minimum."],3],["tf9",0,2,"A developer runs terraform init and gets a newer provider patch than a teammate did last week. What stops this drift between machines?",["Committing the .terraform directory","Deleting the local provider plugin cache before every run","Setting TF_LOG to TRACE","Committing the .terraform.lock.hcl file"],3,"The dependency lock file records the selected provider versions and checksums; committed to VCS, everyone gets the same builds. The .terraform directory holds downloaded plugins and should not be committed.","Objective 2a",["The .terraform directory holds downloaded plugins and modules for one machine and should be ignored by Git; it does not pin versions for others.","Clearing the plugin cache just forces a fresh download, which would still pick the newest matching version without a lock file.","TF_LOG only controls log verbosity and has no effect on which provider version is chosen.",null],2],["tf10",0,2,"The lock file pins aws 5.40.0, and the constraint ~> 5.40 would allow 5.72. How do you move to the newest allowed version?",["Run terraform validate","Run terraform apply -refresh-only","Run terraform init -upgrade","Delete terraform.tfstate"],2,"`init -upgrade` ignores the locked versions, picks the newest within the constraints and updates the lock file. Validate and refresh-only don't touch providers.","Objective 2a",["terraform validate only checks configuration syntax and consistency; it never downloads or changes provider versions.","A refresh-only apply updates state to match real infrastructure and does not touch providers or the lock file.",null,"Deleting the state file loses Terraform's record of your resources and does nothing to the lock file's provider versions."],3],["tf11",0,2,"What is a Terraform provider?",["A file that stores the current resource attributes","A plugin that lets Terraform call a platform's API","A reusable folder of resources with inputs","The company hosting your remote state"],1,"Providers are plugins that implement resource types and data sources by calling APIs. State stores attributes; modules are reusable folders.","Objective 2b",["That describes the state file, which records resource attributes; providers are the plugins that manage those resources.",null,"A reusable folder of resources with input variables is a module, not a provider.","The company or service that stores remote state is a backend; a provider is a plugin that talks to a platform's API."],1],["tf12",0,2,"A configuration has provider \"aws\" { region = \"us-east-1\" } and provider \"aws\" { alias = \"west\" region = \"us-west-2\" }. How does a resource use the second one?",["provider = aws.west","region = aws.west","provider = \"aws-west\"","alias = \"west\" on the resource"],0,"The `provider` meta-argument takes an unquoted reference `<name>.<alias>`. Resources don't take an `alias` argument.","Objective 2c",[null,"region is an ordinary provider or resource argument that expects a string; it cannot select a provider configuration.","The provider meta-argument takes an unquoted reference in the form aws.west, not a quoted string like \"aws-west\".","alias is set only in the provider block; a resource chooses a configuration with the provider meta-argument, not an alias argument."],2],["tf13",0,2,"source = \"hashicorp/random\" is shorthand for which full provider address?",["github.com/hashicorp/random","releases.hashicorp.com/providers/random","registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/random","app.terraform.io/hashicorp/random"],2,"When no hostname is given, Terraform uses the public registry at registry.terraform.io.","Objective 2a",["Provider source addresses default to the public Terraform Registry, not GitHub, even though many providers are developed there.","releases.hashicorp.com hosts binary downloads, but it is not the default hostname in a provider source address.",null,"app.terraform.io is HCP Terraform's hostname, used for private registry providers only when written out explicitly."],1],["tf14",0,2,"Which is NOT a purpose of Terraform state?",["Storing the provider plugin binaries","Mapping resources in code to real object IDs","Tracking metadata such as dependencies","Caching attributes to speed up planning"],0,"Provider binaries live in the .terraform directory or a plugin cache. State maps real objects, tracks metadata and caches attributes.","Objective 2d",[null,"Mapping configuration to real object IDs is a core purpose of state, so it cannot be the exception.","Tracking metadata such as dependencies (for correct destroy ordering) is one of state's documented purposes.","Caching resource attributes to speed up plans on large infrastructures is one of state's documented purposes."],2],["tf15",0,2,"A database password is set through a sensitive variable. Where can the value still be read in plain text?",["In the terraform plan console output","In the output of terraform fmt","Nowhere; sensitive values are encrypted","In the terraform.tfstate file"],3,"`sensitive` only redacts CLI display. The value is still stored in state, so state must be protected.","Objective 2d",["Terraform redacts sensitive values in plan output, showing (sensitive value) instead of the password.","terraform fmt only reformats configuration files and prints file names; it does not output variable values.","sensitive = true only hides values in CLI output; it does not encrypt them, and they remain readable in state.",null],2],["tf16",0,2,"A terraform block contains required_version = \">= 1.12.0\". An engineer runs Terraform 1.9. What happens?",["Terraform upgrades itself automatically","Terraform returns an error before doing any work","The setting only affects providers","Terraform continues the run but prints a version warning first"],1,"`required_version` constrains the Terraform CLI version; a version outside the range makes Terraform stop with an error.","Objective 2a",["Terraform never upgrades itself; you must install a CLI version that satisfies the constraint.",null,"required_version constrains the Terraform CLI itself; provider versions are set in required_providers.","A version outside the required_version range is a hard error, not a warning, and Terraform will not continue the run."],2],["tf17",0,2,"Which registry tier describes providers owned and maintained by HashiCorp?",["Partner","Official","Community","Archived"],1,"Official providers are owned and maintained by HashiCorp. Partner providers are maintained by technology partners, and community providers by individuals or groups.","Objective 2b",["Partner providers are written and maintained by third-party technology companies, not by HashiCorp.",null,"Community providers are published by individuals or groups in the community, not maintained by HashiCorp.","Archived marks providers that are no longer maintained; it is not the tier for providers HashiCorp actively owns."],1],["tf18",0,3,"An engineer clones a repository with Terraform code and wants to run a plan. What must run first?",["terraform apply","terraform refresh","terraform fmt","terraform init"],3,"init sets up the backend and downloads providers and modules; plan fails without it.","Objective 3b",["apply also needs initialized providers and a backend, so it cannot run before init either.","terraform refresh (deprecated) also needs an initialized directory, and it does not install providers or modules.","fmt can reformat files without init, but it does not prepare the directory, so plan would still fail.",null],1],["tf19",0,3,"Which step in the core workflow lets a reviewer see exactly what will be created, changed or destroyed?",["Plan","Write","Init","Validate"],0,"`terraform plan` compares configuration with state and real infrastructure and shows proposed actions.","Objective 3a",[null,"Write is where you author configuration; it does not compare anything against real infrastructure or show proposed actions.","init prepares the working directory by installing providers and modules; it does not calculate any changes.","validate only checks syntax and internal consistency and never shows what will be created, changed or destroyed."],1],["tf20",0,3,"A pipeline must fail if any .tf file is not in canonical format, without rewriting files. Which command fits?",["terraform validate -json -no-color","terraform plan -lock=false","terraform fmt -check -recursive","terraform init -reconfigure"],2,"`fmt -check` exits non-zero when files need formatting and changes nothing; `-recursive` includes subdirectories. validate checks correctness, not style.","Objective 3g",["validate checks that the configuration is correct, not that it is formatted; -json and -no-color only change the output format.","plan -lock=false runs a plan without state locking; it does not check formatting and is unsafe to use by default.",null,"init -reconfigure resets backend configuration and has nothing to do with file formatting."],2],["tf21",0,3,"What does terraform validate check?",["That the provider credentials are valid and have enough permissions","That resources exist in the cloud account","That the state file matches reality","That the syntax and references are internally consistent"],3,"validate checks syntax, types and references in the configuration without calling provider APIs or reading remote state.","Objective 3c",["validate does not contact provider APIs, so it cannot test credentials or permissions; plan or apply would reveal those problems.","validate does not query the cloud account, so it cannot tell whether resources exist.","Comparing state with reality is what a refresh during plan (or plan -refresh-only) does; validate does not read remote state.",null],1],["tf22",0,3,"A team wants CI to apply exactly the plan a reviewer approved. Which pair of commands does this?",["terraform plan, then a separate terraform apply -auto-approve","terraform validate, then terraform apply","terraform plan -out=tfplan, then terraform apply tfplan","terraform refresh, then terraform apply"],2,"A saved plan file freezes the reviewed actions and apply runs it without a prompt. A fresh apply could produce a different plan.","Objective 3d",["A separate apply computes a fresh plan, which may differ from the one reviewed if code or infrastructure changed in between.","validate produces no plan to review, and a plain apply creates a new plan at apply time.",null,"terraform refresh only updates state and is deprecated; the following apply still builds an unreviewed new plan."],3],["tf23",0,3,"In a plan, a resource is shown with the symbol -/+. What does it mean?",["It will be updated in place without replacement","It will be destroyed and then recreated","It will be read as a data source","It will be imported into state"],1,"-/+ means replacement: destroy then create. ~ is an in-place update.","Objective 3d",["An in-place update is shown with ~; -/+ means the object cannot be updated in place and must be replaced.",null,"Data source reads are shown with <=, not -/+.","Imports appear as an import action in the plan; -/+ shows destroy-then-create replacement."],2],["tf24",0,3,"A container is healthy but you need Terraform to recreate it. What is the current recommended command?",["terraform apply -replace=docker_container.web","terraform taint docker_container.web, then terraform apply","terraform state rm docker_container.web","terraform destroy -auto-approve"],0,"`-replace` plans a replacement for that one resource and shows it in the plan. taint is deprecated; state rm would forget the resource instead.","Objective 3e",[null,"terraform taint still works in some versions but is deprecated, and it marks the resource in state without showing the change in a reviewable plan first.","state rm makes Terraform forget the container while leaving it running; the next apply would try to create a duplicate rather than replace it.","destroy -auto-approve removes every resource in the state, not just the one container, and it does not recreate anything."],2],["tf25",0,3,"How can you preview what terraform destroy would remove without deleting anything?",["terraform destroy -check","terraform validate -destroy","terraform plan -destroy","terraform show -destroy"],2,"`plan -destroy` creates a speculative destroy plan. destroy has no -check option.","Objective 3f",["terraform destroy has no -check option; the way to preview a destroy is plan -destroy.","validate has no -destroy option and never produces a plan of any kind.",null,"terraform show displays existing state or a saved plan; it has no -destroy option to build a destroy preview."],2],["tf26",0,3,"After adding a new module block to the configuration, terraform plan reports \"Module not installed\". What fixes it?",["terraform init","terraform fmt","terraform validate","terraform output"],0,"init (or terraform get) downloads new modules. Any new provider or module requires init.","Objective 3b",[null,"fmt only reformats files; it does not download modules.","validate itself needs modules installed and reports the same missing-module problem rather than fixing it.","terraform output reads output values from state and cannot install modules."],1],["tf27",0,3,"You moved the backend to a new bucket but don't want to copy the old state, just start using the new settings. Which command applies?",["terraform init -upgrade","terraform apply -refresh-only","terraform validate -backend","terraform init -reconfigure"],3,"`-reconfigure` ignores the existing backend setup and configures the new one without migrating state. `-migrate-state` would copy it.","Objective 3b",["init -upgrade updates providers and modules to the newest allowed versions; it does not change how the backend is configured.","A refresh-only apply updates state from real infrastructure; it does not switch backends.","validate has no -backend option, and validate never configures backends.",null],3],["tf28",0,3,"Running terraform apply without a saved plan does what before making changes?",["Applies the changes immediately without showing any output","Creates a plan and asks you to approve it","Runs terraform fmt on all files","Destroys all resources first"],1,"Without a plan file, apply generates a plan and waits for you to type yes (unless -auto-approve is used).","Objective 3e",["Without a saved plan, apply always shows the plan first and asks for approval unless -auto-approve is set.",null,"apply never runs terraform fmt; formatting is a separate command.","apply only makes the changes in the plan; it does not destroy all resources first."],1],["tf29",0,3,"Which plan symbol shows an attribute being updated without replacing the resource?",["+","~","-","<="],1,"~ means update in place. + is create, - is destroy, and <= marks a data source read.","Objective 3d",["+ means a resource will be created, not updated.",null,"- means a resource will be destroyed, not updated.","<= marks a data source being read during the run, not a managed resource update."],1],["tf30",0,3,"A teammate wants to pass a different variable file for staging at plan time. Which syntax is correct?",["terraform plan -tfvars staging","terraform plan -tfvars-file=env/staging.tfvars","terraform plan -input=staging.tfvars","terraform plan -var-file=staging.tfvars"],3,"`-var-file` loads variable values from the named file. The other flags don't exist or mean something else (-input controls prompting).","Objective 3d",["There is no -tfvars option on terraform plan; variable files are passed with -var-file.","-tfvars-file is not a real option; the correct name is -var-file.","-input controls whether Terraform prompts for missing variables (true or false); it does not load a file.",null],2],["tf31",0,3,"Why is terraform destroy considered part of the core workflow rather than just a cleanup step?",["It removes all resources Terraform manages in that state","It rewrites the configuration files","It deletes only the resources that have drifted from the configuration","It removes providers from the lock file"],0,"destroy removes everything tracked in the current state, which is useful for short-lived environments. It doesn't touch code or the lock file.","Objective 3f",[null,"destroy never edits .tf files; the configuration stays the same and can be applied again later.","destroy removes every resource in the state, not only drifted ones; handling drift is done with plan or -refresh-only.","destroy does not touch .terraform.lock.hcl; provider selections stay recorded there."],1],["tf32",0,3,"An engineer runs terraform fmt in a directory. What does it change?",["Resource names to match a naming standard","Deprecated arguments to their current replacement arguments","Indentation and alignment to the canonical style","Provider versions to the latest release"],2,"fmt only rewrites layout (spacing, alignment, indentation). It doesn't rename resources or change logic.","Objective 3g",["fmt never renames resources; renaming would change addresses and needs moved blocks, not formatting.","fmt does not rewrite deprecated arguments; it only changes whitespace and layout, so upgrades are manual.",null,"fmt does not touch provider versions; those change only with terraform init -upgrade."],1],["tf33",0,3,"You want terraform plan to skip checking real infrastructure to speed up a quick review. Which option does that?",["-refresh-only","-lock=false","-parallelism=1","-refresh=false"],3,"-refresh=false uses cached state without querying providers. -refresh-only does the opposite: it plans only state updates from reality.","Objective 3d",["-refresh-only is the opposite: it plans only updates to state from real infrastructure, which requires checking reality.","-lock=false only disables state locking; Terraform still refreshes against real infrastructure.","-parallelism=1 limits concurrent operations, which slows a run down and still performs the refresh.",null],2],["tf34",0,3,"During an outage you need to apply a fix to one resource only. Which option limits the run, and how should it be treated?",["-replace, used for all normal changes","-refresh-only, used after every apply","-target, used only for exceptional cases","-lock=false, used by default in every CI pipeline"],2,"-target focuses plan/apply on given addresses and their dependencies; HashiCorp recommends it only for exceptional situations, not routine use.","Objective 3d",["-replace forces recreation of a resource; it does not limit the run's scope and is not meant for all normal changes.","-refresh-only updates state from reality and makes no infrastructure changes, so it cannot apply a fix.",null,"-lock=false removes protection against concurrent writes and should never be a default; it does not limit scope either."],3],["tf35",0,4,"Given: data \"docker_image\" \"nginx\" { name = \"nginx:latest\" }. How do you reference its ID in a resource?",["docker_image.nginx.id","data.docker_image.nginx.id","data.docker_image[\"nginx\"].id","var.docker_image.nginx.id"],1,"Data sources are referenced as data.TYPE.NAME.ATTRIBUTE. Without the data prefix, the address points to a managed resource.","Objective 4a",["Without the data. prefix, docker_image.nginx refers to a managed resource of that type, which is not declared here.",null,"Brackets with a key are for count or for_each instances; this data source has neither, and the address still needs data.TYPE.NAME.","var. refers to input variables, not data sources, so var.docker_image.nginx.id would point to an undeclared variable."],1],["tf36",0,4,"What is the main difference between a resource block and a data block?",["Resources are managed; data blocks only read existing information","Data blocks create objects; resource blocks only read existing information","Data blocks are only allowed inside modules","Resources cannot reference data blocks"],0,"Resource blocks create, update and delete objects. Data blocks read information from providers without managing it.","Objective 4a",[null,"This reverses the roles: resource blocks manage objects and data blocks only read.","Data blocks can be used in any module, including the root module.","Resources commonly reference data sources, for example using a looked-up image ID or AMI."],1],["tf37",0,4,"Given: resource \"local_file\" \"cfg\" { count = 3  filename = \"cfg-${count.index}.txt\"  content = \"x\" }. Which files are created?",["cfg-1.txt, cfg-2.txt, cfg-3.txt","cfg-count.txt three times","cfg-0.txt, cfg-1.txt, cfg-2.txt","cfg-3.txt only"],2,"count.index starts at 0, so three instances get indexes 0, 1 and 2.","Objective 4e",["count.index starts at 0, not 1, so there is no cfg-3.txt.","The ${count.index} template is replaced with the index number, so the literal word count never appears in a file name.",null,"count = 3 creates three instances with indexes 0 to 2, not a single file named after the count."],2],["tf38",0,4,"Given: for_each = toset([\"dev\", \"prod\"]) on a resource. What are each.key and each.value for the first instance?",["\"dev\" and \"dev\"","0 and \"dev\"","\"dev\" and null","\"key\" and \"dev\""],0,"For a set of strings, each.key and each.value are both the element.","Objective 4e",[null,"Numeric indexes like 0 belong to count; with for_each over a set, each.key is the element itself.","each.value is not null for sets; it equals each.key, the set element.","each.key is the element (\"dev\"), not the literal word \"key\"."],2],["tf39",0,4,"A list of five servers is managed with count. Removing the second item recreates or changes several later servers. What change avoids this?",["Add depends_on to every server","Set create_before_destroy on the servers","Use -target so that only one server is applied at a time","Use for_each over a map or set keyed by name"],3,"count addresses instances by index, so removing one shifts the rest. for_each uses stable keys, so only the removed key is affected.","Objective 4e",["depends_on only changes ordering; count still addresses servers by index, so removing one still shifts the rest.","create_before_destroy changes the order of replacement but still replaces the shifted servers.","-target only narrows one run; the shifted indexes remain and later full applies still change those servers.",null],3],["tf40",0,4,"Variable region has default \"us-east-1\". TF_VAR_region=\"eu-west-1\" is set, terraform.tfvars sets \"us-west-2\", and the command includes -var=\"region=ap-south-1\". Which value is used?",["us-east-1 (the default)","ap-south-1 (-var flag)","eu-west-1 (TF_VAR_region)","us-west-2 (terraform.tfvars)"],1,"Command-line -var and -var-file have the highest precedence, then *.auto.tfvars, terraform.tfvars, and environment variables; the default is used only if nothing else sets it.","Objective 4c",["The default is the lowest-precedence source and is used only if nothing else sets the variable.",null,"TF_VAR_ environment variables are low in precedence, below tfvars files and -var options.","terraform.tfvars outranks environment variables but is overridden by -var on the command line."],3],["tf41",0,4,"Given: variable \"ports\" { type = list(number) }. Which value is valid?",["{ http = 80 }","[80, 443]","\"80,443\"","[\"http\", \"https\"]"],1,"list(number) needs an ordered list of numbers. A map, a single string or a list of words cannot convert.","Objective 4d",["A map with a named key is not a list and cannot be converted to list(number).",null,"A single string is not a list; Terraform will not split \"80,443\" into numbers automatically.","The strings \"http\" and \"https\" cannot be converted to numbers, so the list fails the type check."],2],["tf42",0,4,"Which type fits a value like { name = \"web\", port = 8080, public = true } with fixed attribute names of different types?",["map(string)","list(any)","set(string)","object({ name = string, port = number, public = bool })"],3,"An object type has named attributes each with its own type. A map requires all values to be the same type.","Objective 4d",["map(string) needs every value to be a string, but port is a number and public is a bool (Terraform would convert them and lose the types).","list(any) is an ordered list of elements accessed by index, not a set of named attributes.","A set holds unordered unique values of one type and has no named attributes.",null],2],["tf43",0,4,"What does [for s in var.names : upper(s)] return when var.names is [\"a\", \"b\"]?",["[\"A\", \"B\"]","{ a = \"A\", b = \"B\" }","\"AB\"","[\"a\", \"b\", \"A\", \"B\"]"],0,"A for expression in square brackets returns a list with the transformed elements. Curly braces with => would produce a map.","Objective 4e",[null,"A map would need curly braces and a key => value form; square brackets produce a list.","A for expression returns a collection, not a joined string; join() would be needed for \"AB\".","A for expression transforms elements rather than appending new ones, so the originals are not included."],2],["tf44",0,4,"Resource aws_instance.web uses count = 3. How do you output a list of all three IDs?",["aws_instance.web.id","aws_instance.web[3].id","aws_instance.web[*].id","aws_instance[*].web.id"],2,"The splat expression [*] collects an attribute from every instance into a list. Without an index, a counted resource can't be read as one object.","Objective 4e",["A counted resource is a list of instances, so aws_instance.web.id without an index is an error.","Indexes start at 0, so [3] is out of range for three instances, and it would return only one ID.",null,"The splat goes after the resource name (aws_instance.web[*]); aws_instance[*].web is not valid syntax."],2],["tf45",0,4,"A container resource references docker_image.app.image_id in its image argument. What does Terraform infer?",["Nothing, unless you also add an explicit depends_on argument","That both resources must be in one module","That the image must be a data source","An implicit dependency so the image is handled first"],3,"References create implicit dependencies; Terraform orders the graph from them. depends_on is only needed for hidden dependencies.","Objective 4f",["No depends_on is needed; the reference itself creates the dependency.","References work across resources in one module and do not force them into a particular module.","The image can be a managed resource; referencing docker_image.app refers to a resource, not a data source.",null],2],["tf46",0,4,"An application server needs a policy to be attached first, but no argument on the server references the policy. What should you add?",["lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }","count = 1 on the policy","depends_on = [aws_iam_role_policy.app]","A data \"aws_iam_role_policy\" block for the policy"],2,"depends_on declares a dependency Terraform can't see from references. prevent_destroy and count don't affect ordering.","Objective 4f",["prevent_destroy blocks deletion of a resource and has no effect on creation order.","count only controls how many instances exist; it creates no dependency between the policy and the server.",null,"A data block would only read the policy, and the server still would not reference it, so no ordering is created."],2],["tf47",0,4,"Replacing a load balancer certificate causes downtime because the old one is deleted first. Which setting fixes the order?",["lifecycle { ignore_changes = all }","lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }","depends_on = [aws_lb.main] on the certificate resource","lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }"],1,"create_before_destroy makes Terraform create the replacement before destroying the old object. prevent_destroy would block the replacement entirely.","Objective 4f",["ignore_changes = all stops Terraform updating the resource, so the certificate could never be rotated, and it does not change replacement order.",null,"depends_on sets creation order between resources; it does not make the replacement certificate appear before the old one is destroyed.","prevent_destroy would make any plan that destroys the old certificate fail, blocking the replacement."],3],["tf48",0,4,"An autoscaler changes a group's desired_capacity outside Terraform, and every plan tries to reset it. What is the cleanest fix?",["lifecycle { ignore_changes = [desired_capacity] }","Run every terraform apply with -refresh=false from now on","Remove the resource from state","Add depends_on to the autoscaler"],0,"ignore_changes tells Terraform not to plan updates for those attributes after creation. The other options hide or break management.","Objective 4f",[null,"-refresh=false skips reading real values, which hides all drift and can produce wrong plans; it is not a fix for one attribute.","Removing the resource from state means Terraform no longer manages the group at all.","depends_on only affects ordering; it does not stop Terraform planning to reset desired_capacity."],3],["tf49",0,4,"You want terraform plan to fail if var.env is not dev, test or prod. Which block does this?",["A precondition block inside the provider configuration","A check block with an assert","A validation block inside the variable","An output with sensitive = true"],2,"Variable validation blocks reject bad input with your error message. A check block would only warn.","Objective 4g",["Provider blocks do not accept precondition blocks; custom conditions belong in resource lifecycle blocks, outputs and variables.","A check block assertion only produces a warning, so it would not make the plan fail.",null,"sensitive = true on an output only hides its value; it does not validate var.env."],2],["tf50",0,4,"A team wants a continuous health assertion that a website returns HTTP 200, but a failure should warn rather than block the run. Which feature fits?",["A check block","A postcondition","A variable validation","prevent_destroy"],0,"Check block assertions produce warnings and don't stop plan or apply. Postconditions and validations raise errors.","Objective 4g",[null,"A failing postcondition raises an error and stops the run, rather than warning.","Variable validation checks input values, not a website's response, and a failure is an error that stops the plan.","prevent_destroy blocks destroying a resource; it has nothing to do with health checks."],3],["tf51",0,4,"Where do you place a postcondition that verifies an attribute of a resource after it is created?",["Inside the terraform block, under required_providers","Inside the provider block","Inside a locals block","Inside the resource's lifecycle block"],3,"precondition and postcondition blocks go inside a resource's (or data source's) lifecycle block, or in an output for preconditions.","Objective 4g",["The terraform block and required_providers configure versions and providers; custom conditions cannot go there.","Provider blocks configure the provider connection and do not accept postconditions.","locals blocks only define named values and cannot contain precondition or postcondition blocks.",null],2],["tf52",0,4,"A module passes a database password to a provider and the team must keep it out of both plan files and state. Which approach fits Terraform 1.12?",["Mark the variable sensitive = true and rely on CLI redaction","Use an ephemeral variable passed to a write-only argument","Store the password in a local value","Put the password in the output block"],1,"Ephemeral values are never persisted, and write-only arguments accept them without storing them. sensitive only hides display; the value still lands in state.","Objective 4h",["sensitive only hides the value in CLI output; it is still written to the plan file and state.",null,"Local values are ordinary values, so the password would still be stored wherever it is used, including state.","Outputs are saved in state, so putting the password in one makes it more exposed, not less."],3],["tf53",0,4,"What is the recommended way to supply short-lived cloud credentials to Terraform instead of hard-coding them?",["Put the keys in terraform.tfvars in Git","Generate them from Vault or use environment variables","Add them to a README in the repository","Store them as default values in variables.tf with sensitive = true"],1,"Secrets should come from a secrets manager such as Vault (for example dynamic credentials) or the environment, never from committed files.","Objective 4h",["Committing keys in terraform.tfvars puts long-lived secrets into Git history where anyone with repo access can read them.",null,"A README is a public document; secrets in it are exposed to everyone who can read the repo.","sensitive only hides values in output; the default still sits in plain text in variables.tf in version control."],2],["tf54",0,4,"Which command gives an interactive prompt for testing expressions such as cidrsubnet(\"10.0.0.0/16\", 8, 1)?",["terraform show","terraform graph","terraform output -json","terraform console"],3,"terraform console evaluates expressions and functions against the current configuration and state. The result here is 10.0.1.0/24.","Objective 4e",["terraform show prints state or a saved plan; it cannot evaluate an expression you type.","terraform graph outputs a dependency graph in DOT format and does not evaluate functions.","terraform output -json prints existing output values from state and cannot evaluate new expressions.",null],1],["tf55",0,5,"Which module source refers to a local directory?",["source = \"./modules/network\"","source = \"modules/network\"","source = \"local::modules/network\"","source = \"file:modules/network\""],0,"Local paths must begin with ./ or ../; without them Terraform treats the string as a registry address.","Objective 5a",[null,"Without ./ or ../, Terraform treats \"modules/network\" as a registry address, not a local path, and init fails.","local:: is not a valid source prefix; local paths are written as ./ or ../ paths.","file: is not a supported module source scheme; local modules use relative paths starting with ./ or ../."],1],["tf56",0,5,"A module block uses source = \"hashicorp/consul/aws\". Where does Terraform get it?",["From a GitHub repository with that name","From a local folder named hashicorp","From the public Terraform Registry","From the HCP Terraform state"],2,"The NAMESPACE/NAME/PROVIDER format is a registry address on registry.terraform.io.","Objective 5a",["A GitHub source must be written as github.com/... or with git::; a bare NAMESPACE/NAME/PROVIDER is a registry address.","A local folder would need a ./ or ../ prefix; without it the string is not read as a path.",null,"Modules are never loaded from state; HCP Terraform's private registry would need the app.terraform.io hostname in the source."],1],["tf57",0,5,"For which module source can you use the version argument?",["A local path","A plain HTTP URL","A Git repository cloned over SSH","A module from a registry"],3,"The version argument works only for registry sources (public or private). Git sources pin with ?ref= in the URL.","Objective 5d",["Local path modules are read directly from disk and have no versions, so version cannot be used.","Plain HTTP archive sources do not support the version argument.","Git sources pin with ?ref= in the URL; the version argument is not allowed for them.",null],2],["tf58",0,5,"A child module declares variable \"name\". How does the root module give it a value?",["Set TF_VAR_module_name","Declare the same variable in the root module only","Set name as an argument in the module block","Reference module.child.var.name"],2,"Inputs are passed as arguments in the module block. A root variable with the same name isn't passed automatically.","Objective 5b",["TF_VAR_ environment variables only set root module variables, and module_name is not how child inputs are named.","Child modules do not inherit root variables; a root variable of the same name is not passed automatically.",null,"module.child.var.name is not valid syntax; the root can only read a child's outputs, and inputs are set in the module block."],2],["tf59",0,5,"The root module needs the container ID created inside module \"web\". What is required?",["Nothing; reference module.web.docker_container.this.id directly from the root","An output in the child module, read as module.web.OUTPUT_NAME","A data source in the root module","A depends_on in the child module"],1,"Only outputs cross the module boundary. Internal resources of a child can't be referenced directly.","Objective 5b",["Resources inside a child module are private to it and cannot be referenced from the root.",null,"A data source would look up the container by a separate API call, not read it through the module boundary, and is not required.","depends_on only affects ordering and does not expose any value to the root module."],3],["tf60",0,5,"A module should use an aliased provider configuration aws.west. How is it passed?",["providers = { aws = aws.west } in the module block","provider = \"west\" inside the child's resources","region = \"us-west-2\" in the module block","Nothing; aliased provider configurations are inherited by child modules automatically"],0,"The providers map passes specific provider configurations to a child. Only default (unaliased) configurations are inherited automatically.","Objective 5c",[null,"The provider meta-argument takes an unquoted reference, and child resources cannot see the root's aws.west unless it is passed in.","region is not a module block meta-argument; unless the child declares a region variable this is an error, and it does not pass a provider.","Only default (unaliased) provider configurations are inherited automatically; aliased ones must be passed with providers."],3],["tf61",0,5,"A Git-sourced module must stay on tag v1.4.0. Which source is correct?",["git::https://example.com/net.git with version = \"1.4.0\"","https://example.com/net.git#v1.4.0","git::https://example.com/net.git?ref=v1.4.0","git::https://example.com/net.git//v1.4.0"],2,"Git sources select a tag, branch or commit with ?ref=. The version argument doesn't apply to Git, and // selects a subdirectory.","Objective 5d",["version only works with registry sources; Git sources reject it.","Terraform does not select Git tags with a # fragment; a Git ref must be given with ?ref= in a git:: source.",null,"The // syntax selects a subdirectory inside the repository, not a tag."],3],["tf62",0,5,"You raised a registry module's version constraint. Which command downloads the newer module version?",["terraform init -upgrade","terraform validate","terraform fmt","terraform plan -refresh=false"],0,"init -upgrade (or terraform get -update) fetches newer modules allowed by the constraints.","Objective 5d",[null,"validate only checks configuration and never downloads modules.","fmt only reformats files and never downloads modules.","plan uses already installed modules; -refresh=false only skips refreshing state and downloads nothing."],2],["tf63",0,5,"Given: module \"bucket\" { source = \"./modules/bucket\"  for_each = toset([\"logs\", \"data\"])  name = each.key }. How is the logs instance addressed?",["module.bucket[each.key == \"logs\"]","module.logs.bucket","module.bucket[0]","module.bucket[\"logs\"]"],3,"Instances created with for_each are addressed by key in square brackets.","Objective 5c",["each.key is only available inside the module block itself; the address uses the literal key, module.bucket[\"logs\"].","Module instances are not renamed after keys; the module name stays bucket and the key goes in brackets.","Numeric indexes are for count; for_each instances are addressed by their string keys.",null],2],["tf64",0,5,"What is the root module?",["The module in the public registry with the most downloads","The .tf files in the directory where you run Terraform","The first module block in main.tf","A module that holds only provider blocks"],1,"The working directory's configuration is the root module; it can call child modules.","Objective 5b",["Registry popularity has nothing to do with the root module; the root is your working directory's configuration.",null,"A module block calls a child module; the first one is not the root.","The root module can contain any resources, data sources and module calls, not only provider blocks."],1],["tf65",0,6,"No backend is configured. Where does Terraform keep state for the default workspace?",["In .terraform.lock.hcl","In terraform.tfstate in the working directory","In HCP Terraform, which is used automatically when no backend is set","In the provider's cloud account"],1,"The local backend is the default and writes terraform.tfstate in the working directory.","Objective 6a",[".terraform.lock.hcl records provider versions and checksums; it never holds resource state.",null,"HCP Terraform is used only when a cloud block or remote backend is configured; it is never picked automatically.","Terraform keeps its own state; the cloud provider's account holds the real resources, not Terraform's state."],1],["tf66",0,6,"Two engineers run terraform apply at the same time against the same remote state. What prevents corruption?",["The .terraform.lock.hcl dependency lock file","terraform fmt","Provider aliases","State locking by the backend"],3,"Backends that support locking block a second write until the first finishes. The dependency lock file is about provider versions.","Objective 6b",[".terraform.lock.hcl pins provider versions; it does not stop two applies from writing state at once.","terraform fmt only formats files and has no effect on concurrent state writes.","Provider aliases let you use several provider configurations; they have nothing to do with protecting state.",null],2],["tf67",0,6,"A CI job crashed and left the state locked. Nothing else is running. What should you do?",["Run terraform force-unlock with the lock ID","Delete the state file and let the next run rebuild it","Run terraform init -upgrade","Run terraform apply -lock=true"],0,"force-unlock removes a stale lock. Only use it after confirming no run is active; deleting state loses tracking of every resource.","Objective 6b",[null,"Deleting state makes Terraform forget every resource, so the next run would try to create duplicates.","init -upgrade updates providers and modules and does not release a state lock.","-lock=true is already the default; it tries to take the lock and fails again because the stale lock is still there."],2],["tf68",0,6,"Given: terraform { backend \"s3\" { bucket = var.state_bucket } }. What happens at init?",["It works if the variable has a default","Terraform prompts for the bucket later during apply","It fails because backend blocks can't use variables","Terraform silently uses local state"],2,"Backend configuration is read before variables exist, so references aren't allowed. Use -backend-config for dynamic values.","Objective 6c",["Even with a default, variables cannot be referenced in a backend block because backends are set up before variables are evaluated.","Terraform does not defer backend settings to apply; the backend must be fully configured at init.",null,"Terraform does not silently fall back to local state; it reports an error for the invalid backend configuration."],2],["tf69",0,6,"Each environment uses a different state bucket, but the team wants one backend block. What does Terraform support?",["Interpolating terraform.workspace into the backend bucket name","Setting the bucket with TF_VAR_bucket","A for_each on the backend block","Partial configuration with -backend-config at init"],3,"Partial backend configuration leaves out settings and supplies them from a file or key=value with -backend-config.","Objective 6c",["Backend blocks cannot contain expressions such as terraform.workspace, so it cannot be interpolated into the bucket name.","TF_VAR_ variables set input variables, which backend blocks cannot use.","for_each is a meta-argument for resources and modules; backend blocks do not support it.",null],3],["tf70",0,6,"You added a remote backend to a project that already has local state. Which command moves the existing state?",["terraform state push","terraform apply -refresh-only","terraform init -migrate-state","terraform workspace new remote"],2,"init -migrate-state copies existing state into the newly configured backend after confirmation.","Objective 6c",["state push overwrites remote state with a local file by hand; it is a low-level recovery tool, not the normal way to move to a new backend.","A refresh-only apply updates state from real infrastructure and does not move state between backends.",null,"Creating a workspace starts a new, empty state; it does not copy existing local state to the remote backend."],2],["tf71",0,6,"Someone changed a container's settings with the Docker CLI. How do you see the drift without proposing changes to infrastructure?",["terraform validate","terraform plan -refresh-only","terraform fmt -diff","terraform state rm docker_container.web"],1,"A refresh-only plan shows how state would change to match reality without planning infrastructure updates.","Objective 6d",["validate checks configuration only and never looks at the real container.",null,"fmt -diff shows formatting changes to .tf files, not drift in real infrastructure.","state rm makes Terraform forget the container instead of showing what changed, and it changes state."],2],["tf72",0,6,"A refresh-only plan shows a tag was edited manually, and the team decides the manual value is correct. What should they do?",["Apply the refresh-only plan and update the code to match","Run terraform destroy and then apply again to rebuild from the code","Delete the resource from state","Nothing; drift fixes itself on the next plan"],0,"Applying refresh-only records the new value in state, and updating code keeps the next normal plan from reverting it.","Objective 6d",[null,"Destroying and rebuilding causes downtime and throws away the manual value the team decided is correct.","Removing it from state stops Terraform managing the resource instead of recording the correct value.","Drift does not fix itself; the next normal plan would try to revert the tag to the value in code."],3],["tf73",0,6,"Why should state files never be committed to a Git repository?",["Git cannot store JSON files","Terraform deletes and regenerates the state file at the end of every run","They may contain secrets in plain text and cause conflicts","They are too large to commit"],2,"State holds resource attributes including sensitive values, and shared editing through Git has no locking.","Objective 6c",["Git stores JSON files without any problem; the concern is what the state contains and how it is shared.","Terraform updates the state file in place (keeping a backup); it does not delete and regenerate it every run.",null,"State files are usually small; size is not the reason to keep them out of Git."],2],["tf74",0,6,"You run terraform workspace new staging with the local backend. Where is the staging state stored?",["terraform.tfstate.d/staging/terraform.tfstate","terraform.tfstate","staging.tfvars",".terraform/workspaces/staging.tfstate inside the working directory"],0,"Non-default CLI workspaces with the local backend keep state under terraform.tfstate.d/<name>/.","Objective 6a",[null,"terraform.tfstate at the root is used only by the default workspace.","A .tfvars file holds variable values, not state, and workspaces do not create one.","The .terraform directory records the currently selected workspace (in an environment file); local workspace state lives in terraform.tfstate.d."],2],["tf75",0,7,"A container was created by hand. Which approach lets you import it and draft configuration in one plan?",["terraform state push with a JSON file","terraform apply -replace on the container","Write a data block for it, then run terraform apply to adopt it into state","An import block and terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf"],3,"Import blocks are planned like other changes, and -generate-config-out writes starter configuration for the imported resource.","Objective 7a",["state push uploads a full state file by hand and does not import real objects or generate configuration.","-replace destroys and recreates a resource Terraform already manages; it cannot adopt one it does not track.","A data block only reads the container; it never adds it to state as a managed resource.",null],3],["tf76",0,7,"Which command lists every resource address tracked in the current state?",["terraform output -json","terraform state list","terraform providers","terraform graph"],1,"state list prints addresses; state show ADDRESS shows one resource's attributes.","Objective 7b",["output -json prints output values, not the list of resources in state.",null,"terraform providers lists the providers the configuration requires, not resource addresses.","terraform graph prints a DOT-format dependency graph of the configuration, not a list of state addresses."],1],["tf77",0,7,"You renamed resource \"local_file\" \"a\" to \"local_file\" \"config\". How do you avoid Terraform destroying and recreating it?",["Add depends_on = [local_file.a] to the renamed local_file.config","Add moved { from = local_file.a  to = local_file.config }","Run terraform fmt","Run terraform init -upgrade"],1,"A moved block tells Terraform the object's address changed, so the plan shows a move. terraform state mv does the same imperatively.","Objective 7b",["depends_on on local_file.a would reference a resource that no longer exists in the configuration, and it does not tell Terraform the object was renamed.",null,"fmt only reformats files; it does not change how Terraform maps the old address to the new one.","init -upgrade updates providers and modules and does nothing about renamed resources."],3],["tf78",0,7,"Terraform should stop managing a DNS record but leave it in place. Which option does that?",["terraform destroy -target on the record","Delete the resource block and run terraform apply as normal","lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }","A removed block with lifecycle { destroy = false }"],3,"A removed block with destroy = false drops it from state without deleting it. Deleting the block and applying would destroy it.","Objective 7b",["destroy -target deletes the record, which is the opposite of leaving it in place.","Deleting the block without a removed block makes Terraform plan to destroy the record.","prevent_destroy keeps Terraform managing the record and blocks deletion; it does not stop management.",null],3],["tf79",0,7,"A provider fails with an unclear error and you need detailed logs saved to a file. Which settings do this?",["TF_LOG=DEBUG and TF_LOG_PATH=./tf.log","TF_VAR_log=debug","terraform plan -verbose > ./tf.log 2>&1","TF_INPUT=0"],0,"TF_LOG sets the log level and TF_LOG_PATH writes to a file. There is no -verbose flag on plan.","Objective 7c",[null,"TF_VAR_ variables set input variables; TF_VAR_log would only set a variable named log, not logging.","terraform plan has no -verbose flag; redirecting output captures normal output, not debug logs.","TF_INPUT=0 disables interactive prompts and has nothing to do with logging."],1],["tf80",0,7,"You only want detailed logs from the provider plugin, not from Terraform core. Which variable should you set?",["TF_LOG_CORE","TF_CLI_ARGS_plan","TF_LOG_PROVIDER","TF_WORKSPACE"],2,"TF_LOG_PROVIDER sets the provider log level separately; TF_LOG_CORE does the same for core.","Objective 7c",["TF_LOG_CORE sets the log level for Terraform core, the part you want to leave out.","TF_CLI_ARGS_plan adds extra arguments to terraform plan and does not control logging.",null,"TF_WORKSPACE selects the workspace to use and has nothing to do with logging."],2],["tf81",0,7,"Which command shows a human-readable view of the whole current state or a saved plan file?",["terraform validate","terraform fmt","terraform login","terraform show"],3,"terraform show reads state (or a plan file) and prints it; -json gives machine-readable output.","Objective 7b",["validate only checks configuration and prints validation results, not state or plan contents.","fmt reformats configuration files and does not read state.","terraform login stores an HCP Terraform API token and does not display state.",null],1],["tf82",0,8,"A developer wants to run plans from their laptop but have them execute and store state in HCP Terraform. Which workflow is this?",["VCS-driven","API-driven","CLI-driven","Agent-only"],2,"The CLI-driven workflow starts runs from local terraform commands and executes them remotely.","Objective 8a",["VCS-driven runs start from commits and pull requests in a connected repository, not from terraform commands on a laptop.","API-driven runs are started by tools calling the HCP Terraform API, not from local terraform commands.",null,"\"Agent-only\" is not an HCP Terraform workflow; agents are where runs execute, not a way of starting them."],1],["tf83",0,8,"Which block connects a configuration to an HCP Terraform organization and workspace?",["provider \"hcp\" { organization = \"acme\" workspace = \"app\" }","cloud { organization = \"acme\" workspaces { name = \"app\" } }","backend \"local\" { organization = \"acme\" }","module \"cloud\" { source = \"app.terraform.io\" }"],1,"The cloud block in the terraform block sets the organization and workspaces, then terraform init connects to them.","Objective 8a",["There is no hcp provider setting for organization and workspace here; connecting to HCP Terraform is done with the cloud block, not a provider.",null,"The local backend stores state on disk and has no organization setting.","A module block calls a module; it cannot connect the configuration to HCP Terraform."],2],["tf84",0,8,"The same cloud credentials must be available to 30 workspaces. What is the best HCP Terraform feature for this?",["A variable set applied to them or their project","Copying the same variables into each workspace by hand","A terraform.tfvars file in each repo","A shared module output"],0,"Variable sets share variables across many workspaces or projects from one place.","Objective 8d",[null,"Copying variables by hand into 30 workspaces is error-prone and must be repeated whenever the credentials change.","tfvars files are committed with code, which puts credentials in the repository, and they must be maintained in every repo.","Module outputs pass values within one configuration; they cannot share credentials across workspaces."],2],["tf85",0,8,"How do HCP Terraform projects help a platform team?",["They replace workspaces and keep one shared state file for every environment","They store provider binaries for teams","They group workspaces so access and settings apply to the group","They are needed to run terraform fmt"],2,"Projects organize workspaces and let you grant team access at project level. Each workspace still has its own state.","Objective 8c",["Projects do not replace workspaces; each workspace in a project still keeps its own separate state.","Projects do not store provider binaries; providers come from registries, including the private registry.",null,"terraform fmt runs locally and needs no HCP Terraform project."],2],["tf86",0,8,"A security team needs every run blocked if a storage bucket is public. Which HCP Terraform feature enforces this?",["Policy as code with Sentinel or OPA","Run triggers","Cost estimation","The private module and provider registry"],0,"Policies evaluate each plan and can block runs that break rules.","Objective 8b",[null,"Run triggers start a run in one workspace after another workspace applies; they do not block anything.","Cost estimation shows the projected cost of a plan but does not check bucket settings or block runs by itself.","The private registry shares approved modules and providers but does not enforce rules on each run."],2],["tf87",0,8,"The network workspace changes, and the app workspace that reads its outputs must then plan automatically. Which feature connects them?",["Variable sets","Health assessments","Team tokens","Run triggers"],3,"Run triggers queue a run in one workspace after a successful apply in a source workspace.","Objective 8d",["Variable sets share variable values across workspaces; they do not start runs when another workspace changes.","Health assessments check for drift on a schedule; they do not queue runs after another workspace applies.","Team tokens authenticate API access for a team; they do not link workspaces or start runs.",null],3],["tf88",0,8,"Which HCP Terraform feature periodically checks whether real infrastructure still matches a workspace's state?",["terraform fmt -check","Health assessments (drift detection)","The private module registry","Speculative plans on every pull request"],1,"Health assessments run drift detection and continuous validation of checks on a schedule.","Objective 8b",["fmt -check checks file formatting only and never looks at real infrastructure.",null,"The private registry shares modules and providers and does not check for drift.","Speculative plans preview pull request changes; they run on code changes, not on a schedule to detect drift."],2],["tf89",0,8,"What does a VCS-driven workspace do when someone opens a pull request against its branch?",["Applies the change right away to the workspace's infrastructure","Runs a speculative plan and reports it on the pull request","Deletes the workspace state","Locks the repository"],1,"Speculative plans show the impact of a pull request without applying. Apply happens after merge (with approval if required).","Objective 8a",["VCS-driven workspaces do not apply pull requests; applies happen only after changes are merged into the tracked branch.",null,"Opening a pull request never deletes workspace state.","HCP Terraform does not lock the repository; it only reports the plan result back on the pull request."],2],["tf90",0,8,"Which command stores an API token so the CLI can authenticate with HCP Terraform?",["terraform init -token","terraform workspace select","terraform providers lock","terraform login"],3,"terraform login opens a browser to create a token and saves it in the CLI credentials file.","Objective 8a",["terraform init has no -token option; credentials are stored with terraform login or configured in the CLI config file.","workspace select switches to a different workspace and does not authenticate.","providers lock updates the dependency lock file with provider checksums and does not authenticate to HCP Terraform.",null],1],["x1",0,1,"Long-lived web servers are patched by hand and slowly drift apart until no two are alike. Which approach, commonly paired with Terraform, avoids this?",["Build a new image and replace the servers","Log in and patch each server on a schedule","Take a snapshot of one server and restore it on failure","Store the patch list in the Terraform state"],0,"Immutable infrastructure replaces servers with new ones built from a known image instead of changing them in place, so every instance matches. Scheduled patching is tempting because it is routine, but manual changes are what cause the drift.","Domain 1: Immutable infrastructure",[null,"Patching in place by hand is exactly what lets servers drift apart over time.","Snapshots help recovery but do not stop running servers from changing independently.","State records managed resource attributes; it is not a place to track or apply OS patches."],2],["x2",0,1,"A team needs to create VMs and networks, then install and configure packages inside those VMs. How are Terraform and a configuration management tool usually divided?",["Terraform installs the packages inside the VMs; the other tool creates the VMs and networks","Terraform provisions the infrastructure; the other tool configures software inside it","Terraform replaces configuration management tools entirely","Both tools must manage the same VMs' lifecycle"],1,"Terraform's strength is provisioning and managing the lifecycle of infrastructure, while tools such as Ansible configure what runs inside servers. Saying Terraform replaces them is tempting, but it is not designed for in-guest configuration.","Domain 1: Terraform vs configuration management",["This reverses the usual roles: Terraform is built for provisioning infrastructure through APIs.",null,"Terraform can pass startup scripts, but it is not designed to manage software inside long-lived servers.","Having two tools own the same resources' lifecycle causes conflicts and drift."],2],["x3",0,1,"An engineer changes a firewall rule directly in the cloud console during an incident. On the next terraform plan, Terraform proposes to undo the change. What is the best follow-up?",["Stop running plans for that resource","Keep making the change in the console after each apply","Put the change into the Terraform code and apply it","Delete the state file so the change is kept"],2,"With IaC the code is the source of truth, so an emergency change must be written back into the configuration and reviewed. Ignoring the resource is tempting as a quick fix, but it lets code and infrastructure silently diverge.","Domain 1: IaC as source of truth",["Ignoring the resource leaves code and reality out of sync and hides future drift.","This repeats the drift every time and defeats IaC.",null,"Deleting state makes Terraform lose track of every resource, which is far worse than the drift."],2],["x4",0,1,"An auditor asks who changed a load balancer's settings last month and why. Which IaC practice answers this most easily?",["Taking weekly screenshots of the cloud console","Giving every engineer the same admin account","Running terraform fmt before each commit","Version-controlled configuration with commit history"],3,"Keeping infrastructure code in version control gives an audit trail of each change, its author and the review discussion. terraform fmt is tempting because it is a good habit, but it only formats files.","Domain 1: Advantages of IaC",["Screenshots show a state at a moment but not who made a change or why.","A shared account hides who made each change.","fmt only standardizes code style; it records nothing about who changed what.",null],1],["x5",0,1,"A configuration uses count = 5 for a group of identical servers. You change it to count = 3 and run apply. What does Terraform do?",["Creates three new servers alongside the five","Fails because the count cannot shrink","Destroys two servers so that three remain","Leaves all five until they are deleted by hand"],2,"Terraform is declarative: you state the desired end result and it works out the steps, here removing the two highest-indexed instances. Creating three more is tempting if you think of it as a command to run, but Terraform compares against what exists.","Domain 1: Declarative configuration",["Terraform compares desired and current state; it does not add the new count on top.","count can be lowered; Terraform removes the extra instances.",null,"Terraform manages the end state, so it removes resources no longer in the configuration."],2],["x6",0,2,"A required_providers entry uses version = \"~> 1.2.0\". Which provider version may terraform init select?",["1.2.9","1.3.0","1.1.9","2.0.0"],0,"The pessimistic operator ~> 1.2.0 means >= 1.2.0 and < 1.3.0, so only patch releases are allowed. 1.3.0 is tempting because ~> 1.2 (two parts) would allow it, but three parts pin the minor version.","Domain 2: Version constraints",[null,"~> 1.2.0 only lets the last number rise, so 1.3.0 is outside the range.","Versions below 1.2.0 are excluded, because ~> 1.2.0 also means at least 1.2.0.","A new major version is well outside ~> 1.2.0."],2],["x7",0,2,"A provider constraint is version = \">= 2.0.0, != 2.3.1, < 3.0.0\". Which version satisfies it?",["2.3.1","3.0.0","1.9.9","2.4.0"],3,"Comma-separated constraints must all be true; 2.4.0 is at least 2.0.0, is not 2.3.1 and is below 3.0.0. 2.3.1 is tempting because it sits within the range, but != removes it.","Domain 2: Version constraint operators",["2.3.1 is explicitly excluded by the != condition.","The < 3.0.0 condition excludes 3.0.0.","The >= 2.0.0 condition excludes anything below 2.0.0.",null],2],["x8",0,2,"What does the .terraform.lock.hcl file record?",["The exact version of every module the configuration calls","The selected provider versions and their checksums","The current resource attributes","Which user holds the state lock"],1,"The dependency lock file stores each provider's chosen version, its constraints and checksums so every run uses the same builds. Module versions are tempting to include, but Terraform does not lock modules there.","Domain 2: Dependency lock file",["The lock file tracks providers only; module versions are not recorded in it.",null,"Resource attributes belong in the state file, not the dependency lock file.","State locks are held by the backend; the lock file is about dependencies."],2],["x9",0,2,"Developers run terraform init on macOS and commit the lock file. The Linux CI runner then fails because the lock file has no checksums for its platform. What is the best fix?",["Add .terraform.lock.hcl to .gitignore","Run terraform providers lock with -platform for each OS and commit it","Run terraform init -upgrade on every CI run","Commit the .terraform directory with the macOS plugins"],1,"terraform providers lock -platform=... adds checksums for every listed platform, so one committed lock file works on all of them. Ignoring the lock file is tempting because the error goes away, but consistent provider versions are lost.","Domain 2: Dependency lock file across platforms",["Ignoring the lock file removes version pinning, so machines can drift to different provider builds.",null,"-upgrade picks newer versions each run, which defeats the purpose of locking.","The macOS plugin binaries cannot run on Linux, and .terraform should not be committed."],3],["x10",0,2,"A configuration uses resources from a partner provider published under the namespace mongodb, but has no required_providers entry for it. terraform init fails to find the provider. Why?",["Partner providers must be downloaded by hand","Only official providers can be used without HCP Terraform","The provider block is missing an alias","Without a source, Terraform assumes the hashicorp namespace"],3,"If a provider is not declared in required_providers, Terraform treats its local name as hashicorp/<name>, so providers from other namespaces need an explicit source. The alias answer is tempting because it is also provider syntax, but it has nothing to do with installation.","Domain 2: required_providers source addresses",["Partner providers are served by the Terraform Registry and installed by init like any other.","Any registry provider can be used with the plain CLI.","alias is for multiple configurations of one provider; it does not tell init where to download from.",null],3],["x11",0,2,"In the Terraform Registry, a provider is written and maintained by a third-party technology company that works with HashiCorp. Which tier is it?",["Partner","Official","Community","Archived"],0,"Partner providers are maintained by third-party companies against their own APIs as part of HashiCorp's partner program. Official is tempting, but it is reserved for HashiCorp-maintained providers.","Domain 2: Provider tiers",[null,"Official providers are owned and maintained by HashiCorp itself.","Community providers are published by individuals or groups, not verified partner companies.","Archived describes a provider that is no longer maintained, not who maintains it."],1],["x12",0,2,"A configuration declares both the docker and random providers in required_providers. What does a single terraform init do?",["Downloads only the first provider listed","Fails because one configuration allows one provider","Downloads both provider plugins","Asks you to choose which provider to install"],2,"terraform init reads all provider requirements and installs every plugin the configuration needs. Believing a configuration is limited to one provider is the tempting misconception.","Domain 2: Using several providers",["init installs every provider the configuration needs, not just the first.","A configuration can use many different providers at once.",null,"init does not prompt for providers; it installs all required ones."],1],["x13",0,3,"CI runs `terraform plan -out=tfplan`, and a later step runs `terraform apply -var=\"env=prod\" tfplan`. The apply fails. Why?",["Saved plans expire after one hour","apply needs -auto-approve to use a plan file","The plan file must be named terraform.tfplan","Variables cannot be set when applying a saved plan"],3,"A saved plan already contains the variable values used when it was created, so Terraform rejects -var or -var-file at apply time. The approval flag is tempting to blame, but applying a saved plan never asks for approval.","Domain 3: Saved plans",["Terraform does not put a time limit on saved plans; staleness depends on state changes.","Applying a saved plan does not prompt for approval, so -auto-approve is not needed.","Any file name given to -out can be applied.",null],3],["x14",0,3,"Which terraform apply option skips the interactive approval prompt?",["-input=false","-refresh=false","-auto-approve","-lock=false"],2,"-auto-approve applies without asking for confirmation, which is common in automation. -input=false is tempting because it also disables prompts, but not the approval step.","Domain 3: terraform apply options",["-input=false stops prompts for missing variable values, but apply still requires approval.","This skips refreshing state; it does not affect the approval prompt.",null,"This disables state locking, which is risky and unrelated to approval."],1],["x15",0,3,"A CI job must tell three results apart: plan succeeded with no changes, plan succeeded with changes, or plan errored. Which option makes terraform plan report this through its exit code?",["-json","-detailed-exitcode","-compact-warnings","-no-color"],1,"With -detailed-exitcode, plan exits 0 for no changes, 1 for an error and 2 for a successful plan with changes. -json is tempting for automation, but it does not change what the exit code means.","Domain 3: terraform plan in automation",["-json changes the output format, but the exit code alone does not distinguish changes from no changes.",null,"This only shortens how warnings are shown.","This removes color codes from the output; exit codes stay the same."],2],["x16",0,3,"terraform validate passes, but terraform apply fails because the cloud rejects an image ID that does not exist. Why did validate not catch it?",["validate does not contact provider APIs","validate only checks the formatting style","validate only runs after a plan","validate skips resources that use variables"],0,"terraform validate checks syntax and internal consistency without calling remote services, so it cannot know whether an ID really exists. Confusing it with fmt is tempting, but formatting is a separate command.","Domain 3: terraform validate",[null,"Formatting is fmt's job; validate checks syntax, types and references.","validate runs on its own and needs no plan.","validate checks every resource; it just does not check real values against the cloud."],2],["x17",0,3,"You want to see what formatting changes terraform fmt would make, as a diff, before it rewrites files. Which option shows the differences?",["-diff","-list=false","-write=true","-json"],0,"terraform fmt -diff prints the formatting differences (add -check to avoid writing). -list=false is tempting because it controls output, but it only hides file names.","Domain 3: terraform fmt",[null,"-list=false only stops fmt from printing the names of changed files.","-write=true is the default and rewrites files; it does not display the changes.","terraform fmt does not offer a JSON diff output."],1],["x18",0,3,"A provider's API starts returning rate-limit errors when Terraform creates many resources at once. Which option reduces concurrent operations during apply?",["-target on each resource in turn","-parallelism with a lower value","-refresh=false","-lock-timeout with a higher value"],1,"-parallelism sets how many resource operations Terraform runs at the same time while walking the graph. -target is tempting because it reduces the work, but it is not meant for routine use.","Domain 3: Parallelism",["-target limits which resources are included, and is meant for exceptional cases, not rate limits.",null,"This skips the refresh step but does not reduce concurrent create or update calls.","This only changes how long Terraform waits for the state lock."],2],["x19",0,3,"Resource B references an attribute of resource A. Resource C references nothing. In what order does Terraform create them on the first apply?",["A, then B, then C, in file order","All three at once, since none use depends_on","A and C can be created in parallel; B waits for A","C first, then A, then B, alphabetically"],2,"Terraform builds a dependency graph and runs independent nodes concurrently, so A and C start together while B waits for A. Assuming no dependency without depends_on is tempting, but references create implicit dependencies.","Domain 3: Dependency graph",["Terraform follows the dependency graph, not the order of blocks in files.","The reference creates an implicit dependency, so B must wait for A.",null,"Names do not decide order; only dependencies do."],3],["x20",0,3,"Which command is equivalent to terraform destroy?",["terraform plan -destroy","terraform state rm -all","terraform apply -replace","terraform apply -destroy"],3,"terraform destroy is a convenience alias for terraform apply -destroy, which plans and then removes all managed resources. plan -destroy is tempting, but it is only a preview.","Domain 3: terraform destroy",["plan -destroy only previews the destruction and changes nothing.","state rm only forgets resources in state; it does not delete them, and there is no -all flag.","-replace recreates a named resource; it does not destroy everything.",null],1],["x21",0,3,"A plan output shows `<= data.http.config` next to a data source. What does this mean?",["The data source will be destroyed","The data source will be replaced","The data source will be read during apply","The data source is being imported into state"],2,"The <= symbol marks a read action, usually because the data source depends on values not known until apply. Replacement is tempting because it is another special symbol, but that is -/+.","Domain 3: Plan symbols",["Data sources are never destroyed; destruction is shown with -.","Replacement is shown with -/+ for managed resources.",null,"Imports are reported separately in the plan, not with <=."],2],["x22",0,3,"Engineer A saves a plan. Before it is applied, engineer B applies a different change to the same state. What happens when A applies the saved plan?",["Terraform refuses because the saved plan is stale","The plan applies and overwrites B's change silently","Terraform merges both changes automatically","Terraform waits for B's lock to be released and then applies"],0,"A saved plan is tied to the state it was made from; if the state changed, Terraform reports the plan as stale and a new plan is needed. The lock answer is tempting because locking also protects state, but it does not apply here.","Domain 3: Saved plans and state changes",[null,"Terraform checks that state has not changed since planning, so it will not apply blindly.","Terraform does not merge plans; the old plan has to be recreated.","B's run has already finished; the problem is outdated state, not a held lock."],3],["x23",0,3,"What is the difference between terraform plan -refresh-only and terraform plan -refresh=false?",["They are two spellings of the same option","-refresh-only changes infrastructure; -refresh=false does not","-refresh=false updates state from reality; -refresh-only skips checking it","-refresh-only only updates state; -refresh=false skips the refresh"],3,"-refresh-only proposes only state updates to match real infrastructure, while -refresh=false plans against the existing state without querying providers. Reading them as the same option is tempting because of the similar names.","Domain 3: Planning options",["They do opposite things: one only refreshes, the other skips refreshing.","Neither changes infrastructure; a refresh-only apply only updates state.","This reverses them: -refresh-only is the one that updates state from the real world.",null],3],["x24",0,4,"terraform.tfvars sets instance_type = \"t3.small\", and prod.auto.tfvars sets instance_type = \"t3.large\". No -var or -var-file options are used. Which value applies?",["t3.small","t3.large","The variable's default","Neither; Terraform errors on conflicting files"],1,"Terraform loads terraform.tfvars first and then *.auto.tfvars files in lexical order, so prod.auto.tfvars wins. t3.small is tempting because terraform.tfvars sounds like the main file.","Domain 4: Variable value precedence",["terraform.tfvars is loaded before any *.auto.tfvars files, so the later file overrides it.",null,"Defaults apply only when no other value is provided.","Terraform does not error; later sources override earlier ones."],2],["x25",0,4,"A run uses `-var-file=common.tfvars -var-file=prod.tfvars`, and both files set replicas. Which value is used?",["The value from common.tfvars","The value from prod.tfvars","The sum of both values","Terraform prompts for a value"],1,"When the same variable is set more than once on the command line, the last one wins, here prod.tfvars. Choosing the first file is tempting if you assume the earliest setting takes priority.","Domain 4: Variable value precedence",["Later options on the command line override earlier ones, so common.tfvars loses.",null,"Terraform never combines values; one overrides the other.","Terraform only prompts when no value is given at all."],2],["x26",0,4,"Variable size has default = 10 and nullable = false. A module call passes size = null. What value does the module see?",["null","0","An error is raised","10"],3,"When nullable is false and a default exists, passing null makes Terraform use the default. null is tempting because it is what was passed, but nullable = false forbids it.","Domain 4: Input variables (nullable)",["nullable = false means the variable can never be null inside the module.","Terraform does not convert null to zero for numbers.","With a default present, Terraform uses the default instead of raising an error.",null],3],["x27",0,4,"A security group needs one ingress block for each port in var.ports, and the list changes per environment. Which feature generates these nested blocks?",["A dynamic \"ingress\" block with for_each","count on the ingress block","A for expression in a locals block","A module per port"],0,"A dynamic block with for_each generates repeated nested blocks from a collection. count is tempting because it repeats resources, but it cannot be used on nested blocks.","Domain 4: dynamic blocks",[null,"count applies to resources and modules, not to nested blocks inside a resource.","A for expression builds a value, but it cannot create nested blocks on its own.","Separate modules are heavy-handed and would not add nested blocks to one resource."],2],["x28",0,4,"aws_instance.web uses for_each over a map. An output written as aws_instance.web[*].id fails. Which expression returns a list of all the IDs?",["aws_instance.web.*.id[0]","aws_instance.web[\"*\"].id","values(aws_instance.web)[*].id","aws_instance.web[count.index].id"],2,"Resources with for_each are maps of instances, and splat works on lists, so values() turns the map into a list first (a for expression also works). The index answer is tempting because it looks like splat, but maps cannot be indexed by position.","Domain 4: Splat expressions and for_each",["This is the old splat form with an index; it still treats the map like a list and would give one ID at most.","This looks up an instance whose key is literally *, which does not exist.",null,"count.index exists only in resources that use count, not in outputs or for_each resources."],3],["x29",0,4,"var.tags is { env = \"dev\", team = \"web\" }. Which expression builds a map with the same keys and upper-case values?",["[ for k, v in var.tags : upper(v) ]","{ for v in var.tags : v => upper(v) }","upper(var.tags)","{ for k, v in var.tags : k => upper(v) }"],3,"A for expression in braces with key => value produces a map, and two symbols bind each key and value. The square-bracket version is tempting but it returns a list.","Domain 4: for expressions",["Square brackets build a list, so the keys are lost.","With one symbol over a map it binds the value, so the keys become the old values instead of env and team.","upper works on a single string, not on a whole map.",null],2],["x30",0,4,"A monitoring resource should exist only when var.enable_monitoring is true. Which argument is the common pattern?",["depends_on = [var.enable_monitoring]","lifecycle { ignore_changes = [var.enable_monitoring] }","count = var.enable_monitoring ? 1 : 0","enabled = var.enable_monitoring"],2,"A conditional expression setting count to 1 or 0 creates or skips the resource. depends_on is tempting because it references the variable, but it only controls ordering.","Domain 4: Conditional expressions and count",["depends_on sets ordering between objects; it cannot switch a resource on or off.","ignore_changes lists resource attributes to ignore, not a condition for creating the resource.",null,"enabled is not a Terraform meta-argument, so it only works if the provider defines it."],2],["x31",0,4,"A VM should be recreated whenever a separate terraform_data resource that holds a revision number changes, even though no VM argument references it. Which lifecycle argument does this?",["create_before_destroy","replace_triggered_by","ignore_changes","prevent_destroy"],1,"replace_triggered_by replaces the resource when any referenced resource or attribute changes. create_before_destroy is tempting because it concerns replacement, but it only controls ordering.","Domain 4: lifecycle replace_triggered_by",["This changes the order of replacement but does not cause one.",null,"ignore_changes stops attribute changes from triggering updates, the opposite of what is needed.","prevent_destroy blocks destruction and would stop the replacement."],2],["x32",0,4,"A production database has lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }. A change would require replacing it. What happens at plan time?",["Terraform returns an error and the plan fails","Terraform replaces it after asking twice","Terraform skips the database and applies the rest","Terraform updates it in place instead"],0,"prevent_destroy makes any plan that would destroy the resource fail with an error. Expecting Terraform to skip it quietly is tempting, but it stops the whole plan so someone must decide.","Domain 4: lifecycle prevent_destroy",[null,"prevent_destroy does not add confirmations; it blocks the plan.","Terraform does not skip resources silently; the whole plan errors.","Terraform cannot turn a required replacement into an in-place update."],1],["x33",0,4,"A root module output returns var.db_password, which is marked sensitive. terraform plan errors on the output. What is the fix?",["Add sensitive = true to the output","Wrap the value in nonsensitive() by default","Move the output into a locals block","Run plan with -compact-warnings"],0,"Terraform requires outputs that use sensitive values to be marked sensitive themselves, so the value is redacted in CLI output. nonsensitive() is tempting because it makes the error go away, but it exposes the secret.","Domain 4: Sensitive outputs",[null,"nonsensitive removes the protection and should only be used deliberately, not as a routine fix.","Locals are not outputs and cannot be read from outside the module.","This only changes how warnings are displayed; the error remains."],3],["x34",0,4,"What is the key difference between a local value and an input variable?",["Locals can be set with -var on the command line","Locals cannot be set from outside the module","Locals are stored encrypted in state","Locals must be literal strings"],1,"Local values are named expressions inside a module, while input variables are the module's parameters set by callers. The -var answer is tempting, but it applies only to variables.","Domain 4: Local values",["-var sets input variables only; locals are computed inside the module.",null,"Locals are not stored in state as separate values and are not encrypted.","Locals can hold any expression, including references and functions."],1],["x35",0,4,"var.zones is declared with type = set(string). The expression var.zones[0] fails. Why?",["Sets are always empty until apply","Index 0 is invalid; indexes start at 1","Sets are unordered, so their elements cannot be indexed","Sets can only hold numbers"],2,"A set has no order and no duplicates, so it cannot be indexed; convert it with tolist() or use for_each. Blaming the index start is tempting, but lists start at 0 and sets have no index at all.","Domain 4: Complex types (set)",["A set holds values as soon as they are assigned.","Terraform list indexes start at 0; the problem is that sets have no index at all.",null,"set(string) holds strings; sets can have any single element type."],3],["x36",0,4,"A configuration reads a database password from HashiCorp Vault with a data source and passes it to a resource argument. What should the team know about the value?",["It is never stored because it came from Vault","It is encrypted in state with Vault's key","It is written to the configuration files","It is written to the state file, so state must be protected"],3,"Using Vault keeps secrets out of code, but data source results and resource arguments still end up in state unless ephemeral or write-only features are used. Assuming Vault keeps it out of state is the tempting mistake.","Domain 4: Secrets with Vault",["Values read by data sources and used in resource arguments are recorded in state.","Terraform does not encrypt individual values in state; protection comes from the backend.","Terraform never writes values back into .tf files.",null],3],["x37",0,5,"Given: module \"vm\" { source = \"./modules/vm\"  count = 2 }. How is the first instance addressed?",["module.vm[1]","module.vm.0","module.vm[0]","module.vm[\"0\"]"],2,"Modules using count become a list of instances addressed as module.NAME[index], starting at 0. The string key is tempting because it looks like for_each addressing.","Domain 5: count on modules",["count indexes start at 0, so [1] is the second instance.","Dot-number syntax is not how instances are addressed.",null,"String keys are used with for_each; count uses numeric indexes."],1],["x38",0,5,"A module lives in the modules/vpc folder of a Git repository and must be pinned to tag v2.0.0. Which source is correct?",["git::ssh://git@example.com/infra.git//modules/vpc?ref=v2.0.0","git::ssh://git@example.com/infra.git/modules/vpc?ref=v2.0.0","git::ssh://git@example.com/infra.git?ref=v2.0.0 with path = modules/vpc","git::ssh://git@example.com/infra.git//modules/vpc with version = 2.0.0"],0,"A double slash separates the repository from a subdirectory, and ?ref= selects the tag. The version argument is tempting, but it only works with registry sources.","Domain 5: Module sources (Git subdirectories)",[null,"A single slash is read as part of the repository URL, not a subdirectory.","module blocks have no path argument; the subdirectory goes in the source string.","version works only for registry modules; Git sources use ref."],3],["x39",0,5,"You want to download or update modules without initializing the backend or installing providers. Which command does only that?",["terraform init -backend=false -get-plugins=false","terraform providers mirror","terraform validate","terraform get"],3,"terraform get downloads the modules referenced in the configuration into .terraform/modules. The init variant is tempting because init also installs modules, but it does more than that.","Domain 5: Installing modules",["-get-plugins is not a current init option, and init is the broader command.","This copies provider plugins to a local directory; it does not handle modules.","validate checks configuration; it does not download anything.",null],2],["x40",0,5,"A module is published in the private registry of HCP Terraform organization acme as module vpc for provider aws. Which source address is correct?",["acme/vpc/aws","app.terraform.io/acme/vpc/aws","registry.terraform.io/acme/vpc","git::app.terraform.io/acme/vpc"],1,"Private registry sources use hostname/organization/name/provider, and HCP Terraform's host is app.terraform.io. The short form is tempting, but without a hostname Terraform uses the public registry.","Domain 5: Private registry modules",["Without a hostname, Terraform looks in the public registry, not the private one.",null,"This is the public registry host and it omits the provider part of the address.","Private registry modules are not fetched through Git sources."],2],["x41",0,5,"In the standard module structure, where should a module's input variable declarations go?",["outputs.tf","variables.tf","terraform.tfvars","README.md"],1,"The standard layout uses main.tf for resources, variables.tf for inputs and outputs.tf for outputs, plus a README. terraform.tfvars is tempting because it involves variables, but it only sets values.","Domain 5: Standard module structure",["outputs.tf is for output values, not input variables.",null,"tfvars files assign values to variables; they do not declare them, and modules should not rely on them.","The README documents the module but does not declare anything."],1],["x42",0,5,"module \"app\" takes vpc_id = module.network.vpc_id. No depends_on is written. How does Terraform order the two modules?",["It creates them in the order they appear in the files","It creates them in parallel with no ordering","It fails until depends_on is added","It builds resources that feed vpc_id before those that use it"],3,"Referencing a module output creates an implicit dependency, so the resources behind that output are handled first. Thinking depends_on is required is tempting, but it is only needed when no reference exists.","Domain 5: Module outputs and dependencies",["File order does not determine order; references do.","The reference creates an implicit dependency, so they cannot be fully parallel.","depends_on is not required when a reference already exists.",null],2],["x43",0,5,"A shared module will be called with for_each by several teams. Why should it declare required_providers but not contain its own provider configuration block?",["Such modules cannot then be used with for_each, count or depends_on","Provider blocks inside modules are ignored","required_providers is only allowed in child modules","Provider blocks in modules make init download plugins twice"],0,"A module that defines its own provider configuration is not compatible with count, for_each and depends_on, so reusable modules should receive providers from the caller. Saying the blocks are ignored is tempting, but they are used and cause this restriction.","Domain 5: Providers in modules",[null,"They are not ignored; they create a separate provider configuration, which is the source of the limitation.","required_providers is used in root and child modules alike.","Plugins are downloaded once per configuration regardless of where provider blocks live."],3],["x44",0,6,"Several CI jobs share one remote state. When a job finds the state locked, it fails immediately instead of waiting. Which option makes it wait for the lock?",["-lock=false","-parallelism=1","-lock-timeout=5m","-refresh=false"],2,"-lock-timeout tells Terraform to retry acquiring the lock for the given duration before failing. -lock=false is tempting because the error goes away, but it removes the protection.","Domain 6: State locking",["This disables locking, which risks corrupting state when jobs overlap.","This limits concurrent resource operations within a run, not waiting for another run's lock.",null,"This skips refreshing and does not affect lock handling."],2],["x45",0,6,"With the local backend, what is the terraform.tfstate.backup file?",["A copy of the configuration files","A lock file that blocks other runs","An encrypted copy of the current state","The previous version of the state"],3,"The local backend keeps the state from before the last write as terraform.tfstate.backup, which helps recover from mistakes. An encrypted copy is tempting to assume, but it is plain JSON.","Domain 6: Local backend",["State files hold resource data, not .tf configuration.","Local locking does not use this file; it holds the previous state.","The backup is plain JSON, just like the main state file.",null],1],["x46",0,6,"State for a production project is stored in an S3 bucket. Which measures protect it best?",["Make the bucket public so CI can reach it","Commit a copy of the state to Git as a backup","Encryption, versioning and tightly limited access","Mark all variables sensitive so state is encrypted"],2,"State can contain secrets, so remote state should be encrypted at rest, versioned for recovery and restricted to those who need it. Marking variables sensitive is tempting, but it does not encrypt state.","Domain 6: Protecting remote state",["Public access would expose secrets stored in the state.","Git copies spread secrets to everyone with repository access and create conflicts.",null,"sensitive only hides values in CLI output; state still holds them in plain text."],2],["x47",0,6,"A configuration should name a bucket app-dev in workspace dev and app-prod in workspace prod. Which expression does this?",["\"app-${var.workspace}\"","\"app-${terraform.workspace}\"","\"app-${workspace.name}\"","\"app-${path.workspace}\""],1,"terraform.workspace returns the name of the current CLI workspace. var.workspace is tempting, but it is not built in.","Domain 6: CLI workspaces",["This only works if someone declares and sets a workspace variable by hand.",null,"There is no workspace object; the built-in value is terraform.workspace.","path has module, root and cwd, but no workspace attribute."],1],["x48",0,6,"A team wants dev and prod isolated with different credentials and access controls, and plans to use CLI workspaces in one backend. Why is this a weak fit?",["They share one backend and its credentials, so isolation is weak","Workspaces can hold only one resource each","Workspaces cannot use different variable values for each environment","Workspaces are removed by terraform init"],0,"CLI workspaces are several states in the same backend with the same credentials, so they are not suited to strong separation; separate configurations or backends are recommended. The variable answer is tempting, but workspaces can use different values.","Domain 6: CLI workspaces and isolation",[null,"Workspaces can hold any number of resources.","Different values are possible, for example with terraform.workspace or separate tfvars files.","init does not delete workspaces."],3],["x49",0,6,"The network team's configuration exports vpc_id as a root output in its remote state. How can the app configuration read it without copying values by hand?",["A terraform_remote_state data source","A module block pointing at the network state file","terraform state pull in the app configuration","A moved block that links the two states"],0,"terraform_remote_state reads the root module outputs of another state (an HCP Terraform outputs data source also works there). A module block is tempting, but modules are about code, not state.","Domain 6: Reading other state",[null,"Modules take source code, not state files.","state pull prints the raw state of the current configuration; it is not a way to reference another project's outputs.","moved blocks refactor addresses within one configuration."],2],["x50",0,6,"A storage bucket managed by Terraform is deleted manually in the console. What does the next terraform plan show?",["An error that state is corrupt","A plan to create the bucket again","No changes, because state still lists the bucket","A plan to remove the bucket's block from the code"],1,"During refresh Terraform sees the object is gone and, since the configuration still declares it, plans to create it. No changes is tempting if you think plan trusts state alone, but it checks reality first.","Domain 6: Resource drift",["Terraform treats a missing object as drift, not corruption.",null,"Plan refreshes state first, finds the bucket missing, and plans to recreate it.","Terraform never edits your configuration files."],2],["x51",0,7,"You move a bucket resource from the root module into a new child module called logs. How do you keep Terraform from destroying and recreating the bucket?",["removed { from = aws_s3_bucket.logs }","depends_on = [aws_s3_bucket.logs] in the module block","moved { from = aws_s3_bucket.logs  to = module.logs.aws_s3_bucket.this }","Run terraform import again on the old root address after deleting the block"],2,"moved blocks can map a resource to an address inside a module, so the existing object is reused. A removed block is tempting because it also refactors, but it drops management instead of moving it.","Domain 7: Refactoring with moved blocks",["A removed block makes Terraform forget or destroy the object; it does not link it to its new address.","depends_on sets ordering and does not change resource addresses.",null,"Importing to the old address keeps it in the wrong place and does not match the new code."],3],["x52",0,7,"What is the main advantage of a removed block over terraform state rm?",["removed blocks always delete the real resource as well as forgetting it","state rm works only with local state","removed blocks do not need an apply","The change is written in code and shown in a plan before it happens"],3,"A removed block is declarative: it is reviewed in version control and appears in the plan, while state rm changes state immediately. Saying removed deletes the resource is tempting, but destroy = false keeps it.","Domain 7: removed blocks vs terraform state rm",["With lifecycle destroy = false, the object is left in place; neither method must delete it.","state rm works with any backend.","A removed block takes effect on apply, after it appears in the plan.",null],3],["x53",0,7,"A shell script needs the value of output db_host with no quotes or JSON formatting. Which command?",["terraform output -json db_host","terraform show db_host","terraform output -raw db_host","terraform state show db_host"],2,"terraform output -raw prints a single string output exactly as-is, which is ideal for scripts. -json is tempting for automation, but it keeps the quotes.","Domain 7: terraform output",["-json wraps a string in quotes, which the script would have to strip.","terraform show displays state or a plan; it does not take an output name.",null,"state show takes a resource address, not an output name."],1],["x54",0,7,"`terraform import aws_instance.web i-0abc123` fails saying the resource address does not exist in the configuration. What is missing?",["A resource block for aws_instance.web","The -generate-config-out option on import","A data block for the instance","terraform init -upgrade"],0,"The terraform import command attaches an existing object to an address that must already be declared in the configuration. -generate-config-out is tempting, but it works with import blocks in terraform plan.","Domain 7: terraform import command",[null,"-generate-config-out is a plan option used with import blocks, not the import command.","A data block only reads the instance; import needs a managed resource block.","Upgrading providers does not add the missing resource block."],2],["x55",0,8,"How do HCP Terraform workspaces differ from CLI workspaces?",["They are the same as CLI workspaces with a new name","They share one state file for all environments","They can only be used with the VCS-driven workflow","Each has its own state, variables, permissions and run history"],3,"An HCP Terraform workspace is a separate unit with its own state, variables, access and runs. Treating them as renamed CLI workspaces is tempting, but CLI workspaces lack their own variables and permissions.","Domain 8: HCP Terraform workspaces",["CLI workspaces only split state inside one backend; HCP workspaces hold much more.","Each HCP Terraform workspace has its own state.","Workspaces support VCS, CLI and API-driven workflows.",null],2],["x56",0,8,"A workspace needs AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID set for the provider. In HCP Terraform, which variable category fits?",["Terraform variable","Environment variable","Output variable","Local value"],1,"Environment variables are set in the run environment, where providers read credentials such as AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID. Terraform variable is tempting, but that category maps to declared input variables.","Domain 8: Variables in HCP Terraform",["Terraform variables fill input variables in the configuration; the provider reads AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID from the environment.",null,"Outputs are values a workspace exports, not inputs set for runs.","Local values are defined in configuration, not in workspace settings."],2],["x57",0,8,"A variable is marked sensitive in an HCP Terraform workspace. What does that mean for users of the web interface?",["The value is visible only to the workspace creator","The value can be set and replaced but not viewed","The value is not sent to runs","The value is stored in the Git repository"],1,"Sensitive workspace variables are write-only: they can be updated or deleted but not read back through the UI or API. Believing the creator can still see them is tempting, but no one can.","Domain 8: Sensitive variables",["Once saved, a sensitive variable's value is not shown to anyone in the interface.",null,"Sensitive variables are still available to runs; they are just hidden from view.","Workspace variables are stored in HCP Terraform, not in your repository."],1],["x58",0,8,"A team wants HCP Terraform runs to authenticate to the cloud without storing long-lived access keys in the workspace. Which feature fits?",["A variable set holding the access keys","Run triggers","The private registry","Dynamic provider credentials"],3,"Dynamic provider credentials use workload identity tokens to get short-lived credentials for each run, so no static keys are stored. Variable sets are tempting because they share secrets, but they still hold long-lived keys.","Domain 8: Dynamic provider credentials",["A variable set still stores long-lived keys, just in a shared place.","Run triggers start runs when another workspace changes; they do not provide credentials.","The private registry shares modules and providers, not credentials.",null],2],["x59",0,8,"A security team wants a third-party scanning service to check each plan in HCP Terraform and be able to stop the run. Which integration fits?",["Run tasks","Notifications","Run triggers","Health assessments"],0,"Run tasks call external services at set stages of a run and can be configured to block it on failure. Notifications are tempting because they also reach external tools, but they only report.","Domain 8: Run tasks",[null,"Notifications send run events to other systems but cannot block a run.","Run triggers queue runs in other workspaces; they do not call external checks.","Health assessments check drift and conditions on a schedule, not each plan."],2],["x60",0,8,"A cloud block uses `workspaces { tags = [\"app\"] }` instead of a single name. What does this allow from the CLI?",["Running plans in every workspace that has the tag at the same time, on each command","Creating a new organization for each tag","Using any workspace with that tag, chosen with terraform workspace select","Sharing one state file across all tagged workspaces"],2,"With tags, the working directory can use any HCP Terraform workspace with those tags, and you switch between them with terraform workspace select. Running them all at once is tempting, but the CLI targets one workspace per run.","Domain 8: The cloud block",["The CLI works with one selected workspace at a time.","Tags filter workspaces within one organization; they do not create organizations.",null,"Each workspace keeps its own state even when grouped by tag."],3]]);
